{"id":"h043","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"a) The maximum pressure variation P that the ear can tolerate in loud sounds is about 28 N / m^2 (=28 Pa). Normal atmospheric pressure is about 100,000 Pa. Find the corresponding maximum displacement for a sound wave in air having a frequency of 1000 Hz. b) In the faintest sound that can be heard at 1000 Hz the pressure amplitude is about 2.0 × 10^-5 Pa. Find the correspond-ing displacement amplitude.\nIs the correct answer \"1.2 × 10^-5m, 10^-8m\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The proposed answer of 1.2 × 10^-5 m, 10^-8 m is incorrect.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1243},{"text":"The pressure amplitude relates to displacement amplitude by P0 = ρvωs.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-2.3602},{"text":"With ρ≈1.21 kg/m³, v≈343 m/s and ω≈6283 rad/s, ρvω is about 2.6 × 10^6 Pa/m.","confidence":0.93,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-2.2563},{"text":"Part (a) displacement is about 1.1 × 10^-5 m, close to the proposed 1.2 × 10^-5 m.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-0.3774},{"text":"The small gap in part (a) comes from the choice of constants.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-0.2408},{"text":"Part (b) displacement is about 8 × 10^-12 m.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-2.9283},{"text":"The proposed 10^-8 m for part (b) is about 1000 times too large.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.0023},{"text":"A displacement of 10^-8 m would require about 0.03 Pa, not 2 × 10^-5 Pa.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-2.9543},{"text":"The part (b) error alone is enough to make the stated answer incorrect.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.0885}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":3,"J":-0.8,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.9,"relation":"su